<BaseLayout title="Acupressure Safety: Contraindications &amp; Guidelines" articleSchema={true} datePublished="2026-01-15" dateModified="2026-08-19" description="Essential safety information for acupressure practice. Contraindications, who should avoid it, pregnancy safety, and when to consult a doctor." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Safety", href: "/safety/" }]}>
<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
<h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure Safety & Contraindications</h1>
<p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-4">Essential safety information to ensure you practice acupressure safely and effectively. While acupressure is generally safe, certain conditions require caution.</p><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mb-10">Understanding acupressure contraindications is just as important as knowing the points themselves. This section covers who should avoid acupressure, which points are contraindicated during pregnancy, and when to consult a healthcare provider before starting. Always prioritize safety — acupressure is a complementary therapy, not a replacement for medical care.</p>
<section class="mb-8">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What This Section Covers</h2>
<p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Acupressure is one of the safest self-administered therapies available, but it is not risk-free for everyone. Most of the rules are common sense: never press on open wounds, burns, rashes, varicose veins, active infections, or directly over tumors or recent surgical incisions. People taking blood thinners, people with known blood clots, and people with osteoporosis or fragile skin need a gentler, modified technique, or should avoid acupressure altogether until cleared by a physician.</p>
<p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Pregnancy deserves special attention. The points LI4 (Hegu), SP6 (Sanyinjiao), BL60 (Kunlun), and GB21 (Jianjing) are traditionally used in TCM to stimulate uterine contractions and should be avoided during pregnancy. Many other points are widely considered safe with gentle pressure, such as PC6 (Neiguan) for morning sickness and Yintang or HT7 for anxiety. The guides below lay out the details, including which points to avoid and when to seek medical advice first.</p>
</section>
<section class="mb-8">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Are the Key Safety Guidelines?</h2><h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Core Safety Principles</h3>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-3">
<li><strong>Skip injured or irritated skin.</strong> Avoid acupressure over open wounds, burns, rashes, varicose veins, active infections, and known tumors.</li>
<li><strong>Respect recent surgery.</strong> Wait at least 6 weeks after surgery before pressing on or near the incision, or until your surgeon approves.</li>
<li><strong>Be careful with blood thinners.</strong> Use very light pressure, avoid areas with little muscle padding such as the shins, and check your skin for bruising after each session.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid known blood clots.</strong> If you have a deep vein thrombosis or clotting disorder, avoid acupressure entirely until cleared by a physician.</li>
<li><strong>Adapt technique for fragile skin.</strong> People on corticosteroid therapy, elderly individuals, and those with diabetic neuropathy should use very light pressure and inspect the skin afterward.</li>
<li><strong>Take care during pregnancy.</strong> Avoid LI4, SP6, BL60, and GB21, and consult your OB-GYN or midwife before starting.</li>
</ul>
<p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Acupressure is a complement to medical care, not a replacement. See a doctor for unexplained, severe, or worsening symptoms rather than attempting self-treatment.</p>
</section>
<section class="mb-8">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="space-y-4">
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 cursor-pointer">Can acupressure make a condition worse?</summary>
<p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">In most cases, no. The main theoretical risk is stimulating contraindicated points during pregnancy, which could trigger contractions, or pressing too hard and causing bruising or temporary pain.</p>
</details>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 cursor-pointer">Is it safe to use acupressure while on blood thinners?</summary>
<p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Yes, but with caution. Use very light pressure, avoid areas with little muscle padding, and check the skin for bruising after each session. If you develop large or unexplained bruises, stop and consult your doctor.</p>
</details>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 cursor-pointer">Is acupressure safe during pregnancy?</summary>
<p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Yes, when you use appropriate points and gentle pressure. Avoid the contraindicated points listed above and always check with your OB-GYN or midwife first.</p>
</details>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 cursor-pointer">When should I see a doctor instead?</summary>
<p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">If you have unexplained or worsening pain, severe or recurring headaches, chest pain, shortness of breath, unexplained weight loss or fever, sudden changes in bowel or bladder function, or any symptom that concerns you.</p>
</details>
</div>
</section>
